  longDescription:
    "HAPKIDO (합기도, from HAP = 'coordinated/joined,' KI = 'energy/spirit,' DO = 'way') is a HYBRID KOREAN MARTIAL ART founded in the mid-20TH CENTURY by CHOI YONG-SOOL (1904-1986), a Korean who spent 30 years in Japan (1912-1946) as a servant/adopted son of TAKEDA SOKAKU (1859-1943), the LEGENDARY GRANDMASTER OF DAITO-RYU AIKIJUJUTSU (the same art that Ueshiba Morihei used to found AIKIDO). Choi returned to Korea after WWII + began teaching what he called YAWARA + later HAPKIDO — his synthesis of Daito-ryu jujutsu with Korean TAEKKYON-derived KICKS + traditional Korean weapons + subak grappling. Choi's students — JI HAN-JAE (b. 1936, founder of SIN MOO HAPKIDO), SUH BOK-SUB, KIM MOO-HONG, MYUNG JAE-NAM — spread hapkido globally + developed sub-styles. TECHNICAL FOUNDATIONS: Hapkido rests on THREE CORE PRINCIPLES: (1) HWA (harmony) — never oppose force directly; blend with attack. (2) WON (circular motion) — redirect attacks in circles rather than blocking linearly. (3) YU (water principle) — flow around obstacles like water; use no more force than necessary. TECHNIQUE VOCABULARY: (a) JOINT LOCKS (KWANJEOL GISUL) — extensive wrist, elbow, shoulder, and finger locks from Daito-ryu inheritance. (b) THROWS (NAJIGI) — hip throws, sweeps, sacrifice throws similar to judo but often paired with joint locks. (c) DYNAMIC KICKS (BAL CHAGI) — spinning kicks, jump kicks, flying kicks derived from Korean taekkyon + shared with taekwondo — hapkido has one of the widest kicking vocabularies of any martial art (over 200 kicks documented). (d) STRIKES — punches, elbows, knees, palm heels, spear-hand strikes. (e) PRESSURE POINTS (HYEOL) — targeting nerve + acupuncture points for pain compliance + KO. (f) WEAPONS — JANG BONG (long staff), DAN BONG (short stick), JI PANG YI (cane), SSANG JYEOL BONG (nunchaku), JEONG (traditional sword), KAL (knife), YU DO SUL (belt/rope). (g) FALLING TECHNIQUES (NAKPEOP) — extensive breakfalls + rolls essential due to throw-heavy curriculum. TRAINING: DOJANG (Korean dojo); uniform is DOBOK (traditional Korean uniform) + colored belt system (10 KUP → 1 KUP → 1-10 DAN). Signature training drills: PARTNER JOINT-LOCK FLOW (SU BAK), THROW-COUNTER-THROW (JAP GI PILGI), MULTI-ATTACKER SELF-DEFENSE (BANG YOEK). PHILOSOPHY: Emphasizes SELF-DEFENSE over sport; hapkido has NO OLYMPIC OR UNIFIED WORLD CHAMPIONSHIP (unlike taekwondo). Practical combat effectiveness prioritized. FRAGMENTATION: Multiple federations post-Choi: KOREA HAPKIDO FEDERATION (KHF, 1965), WORLD HAPKIDO FEDERATION (WHF), INTERNATIONAL HAPKIDO FEDERATION (IHF), SIN MOO HAPKIDO (Ji Han-Jae), HANKIDO (Myung Jae-Nam variant emphasizing dance-like flow), KOMBATAN, JUNG SUWON, etc. — each with variations. USE BY KOREAN INSTITUTIONS: Widely adopted by KOREAN NATIONAL POLICE, PRESIDENTIAL SECURITY SERVICE, KOREAN MILITARY (SPECIAL FORCES) as core self-defense curriculum since 1960s. NOTABLE PRACTITIONERS: JI HAN-JAE (introduced hapkido to Bruce Lee for his last film 'GAME OF DEATH' 1978); CHUCK NORRIS (initially trained hapkido); JHOON RHEE (taekwondo + hapkido pioneer in US); ROBERT ALEXANDER (10th Dan grandmaster). GLOBAL SPREAD: Practiced in 100+ countries; particularly popular in USA, RUSSIA, GERMANY, BRAZIL, MEXICO, PHILIPPINES. Continues to grow as a comprehensive practical self-defense art.",
